# Rocks and Minerals: The Building Materials of the Crust

> Every landscape is assembled from minerals bound into rocks, and every
> rock records the process that made it. This lesson sorts the crust's
> materials into three families and follows the cycle that recycles them.

## 1. Minerals: The Ingredients

[definition]
A **mineral** is a naturally occurring, inorganic substance with a
definite chemical composition and an orderly crystal structure. A
**rock** is any aggregate of one or more minerals.
[/definition]

| Mineral property | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hardness | Resistance to scratching (Mohs scale 1–10) | Talc 1; quartz 7; diamond 10 |
| Lustre | Surface shine | Metallic galena; glassy quartz |
| Streak | Colour of the powdered mineral | Haematite streaks red |
| Cleavage | Tendency to split along planes | Mica peels into sheets |
| Density | Mass per unit volume | Galena feels unusually heavy |

## 2. The Three Rock Families

@diagram tree id="rock-families" layout="tree" aria="Igneous, sedimentary, and metamorphic rock families"
  Rocks -> Igneous
  Rocks -> Sedimentary
  Rocks -> Metamorphic
  Igneous -> "Granite (intrusive)"
  Igneous -> "Basalt (extrusive)"
  Sedimentary -> "Sandstone, coal, limestone"
  Metamorphic -> "Marble, slate, gneiss"
@/diagram

## 3. Igneous Rocks: Born of Magma

Igneous rocks form when **magma** (below ground) or **lava** (on the
surface) cools and solidifies. They are called the **primary rocks**
because they crystallised first from the cooling planet and every other
family derives from them.

| Type | Cooling place | Rate | Grain | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Intrusive (plutonic) | Deep underground | Slow | Large visible crystals | Granite |
| Extrusive (volcanic) | Surface | Fast | Fine or glassy | Basalt |

[example]
Granite's salt-and-pepper speckle comes from crystals large enough to
see — proof of slow cooling. Basalt, frozen in the open air, is so
fine-grained that its individual crystals are invisible.
[/example]

## 4. Sedimentary Rocks: Layered Archives

Sedimentary rocks form when weathered material is carried away,
deposited in layers, and then compacted and cemented. They alone hold
**fossils** and display bedding.

| Class | Formed from | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanical (clastic) | Fragments of older rocks | Sandstone, conglomerate, shale |
| Chemical | Minerals precipitated from solution | Limestone, rock salt, gypsum |
| Organic | Compacted remains of living things | Coal, chalk |

## 5. Metamorphic Rocks: Changed by Heat and Pressure

**Metamorphism** means change of form: existing rocks recrystallise in
the solid state under intense heat and pressure, growing new minerals
and textures.

| Original rock | Becomes |
| --- | --- |
| Granite | Gneiss |
| Limestone | Marble |
| Shale | Slate |
| Sandstone | Quartzite |

[tip]
Slate splits into such thin sheets that it roofed houses for centuries,
while marble — recrystallised calcite — sparkles in monuments such as
the Taj Mahal.
[/tip]

## 6. The Rock Cycle

@diagram tree id="rock-cycle" layout="tree" aria="The rock cycle from magma back to magma"
  Magma -> "Igneous rock (cooling)"
  "Igneous rock" -> "Sediment (weathering and erosion)"
  Sediment -> "Sedimentary rock (compaction and cementation)"
  "Sedimentary rock" -> "Metamorphic rock (heat and pressure)"
  "Metamorphic rock" -> "Magma again (deep melting)"
@/diagram

[summary]
No rock is permanent: igneous rock weathers into sediment, sediment is
cemented into sedimentary rock, heat and pressure metamorphose it, and
deep burial may melt it back into magma — closing the loop.
[/summary]

## 7. Rocks in the Indian Record and the Economy

- The **Deccan Traps**, erupted about 66 million years ago near the end
  of the Cretaceous, blanket much of peninsular India in flood basalt.
- The **Cuddapah** and **Vindhyan** systems preserve some of India's
  oldest and thickest sedimentary sequences.

| Rock | Chief uses |
| --- | --- |
| Granite | Building stone, monuments, kitchen counters |
| Basalt | Road metal and railway ballast |
| Sandstone | Building stone (Red Fort walls) |
| Limestone | Cement making and flux in iron smelting |
| Marble | Flooring and sculpture (Makrana marble) |
| Slate | Roofing tiles |
| Coal | Fuel and thermal power generation |

## 8. Quick Revision

| Rock | Family | Formed by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Granite | Igneous intrusive | Slow cooling of magma |
| Basalt | Igneous extrusive | Rapid cooling of lava |
| Sandstone | Sedimentary | Cemented sand grains |
| Coal | Sedimentary (organic) | Compacted plant remains |
| Marble | Metamorphic | Recrystallised limestone |
| Slate | Metamorphic | Pressured shale |
